# Namleh Browser Security Bench

Use this before trusting agents with internal or external web workflows.

## Threat Model

Untrusted web content may try to make the agent:

- reveal secrets or private data
- navigate to attacker-controlled URLs
- click destructive controls
- bypass tenant/client boundaries
- ignore system or project instructions
- exfiltrate screenshots, page text, tokens, or files

## Minimum Controls

- deterministic allowlist for browser/tool commands
- URL and origin validation
- no credential capture in scripts or fixtures
- page content treated as untrusted input
- explicit data/action authorization checks in the app
- canary or sentinel tests for prompt-injection attempts
- negative tests for role and tenant denial

## Benchmark Flow

1. Identify the browser workflow and sensitive data/action.
2. Build benign happy-path fixture.
3. Build adversarial fixture with hidden and visible injection text.
4. Run the workflow against both.
5. Confirm the adversarial case cannot change tool choice, target URL, data
   access, or state-changing action.
6. Save evidence only if it contains no secrets or private customer data.

